1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,310
Imagine that tomorrow, we invent
teleportation. You step into a machine.

2
00:00:06,194 --> 00:00:10,964
A few seconds later, you step out on the
other side of the planet. No airplane.

3
00:00:11,094 --> 00:00:13,504
No rocket. No road. Just... there.

4
00:00:14,271 --> 00:00:17,781
Sounds amazing. But what
exactly happened to you?

5
00:00:18,465 --> 00:00:20,075
Did you travel? Were you copied?

6
00:00:20,745 --> 00:00:22,175
Were you destroyed and rebuilt?

7
00:00:23,128 --> 00:00:27,538
Or did we somehow bend space itself
and simply make the distance disappear?

8
00:00:28,472 --> 00:00:31,422
Because "teleportation" is
not actually one technology.

9
00:00:31,732 --> 00:00:35,162
There are several completely
different ways it could work.

10
00:00:35,472 --> 00:00:38,462
And some of them are much
more disturbing than others.

11
00:00:41,052 --> 00:00:45,402
Let's start with one of the
simplest ideas. The Portal approach.

12
00:00:46,313 --> 00:00:49,903
You don't disassemble anything.
You don't scan anything.

13
00:00:50,533 --> 00:00:52,323
You simply connect two locations.

14
00:00:53,325 --> 00:00:55,915
Imagine that space
normally looks like this.

15
00:00:56,645 --> 00:00:59,875
And somehow, we create a
shortcut. You walk through it.

16
00:01:00,569 --> 00:01:05,639
From your perspective, you are still the
same physical object. You were not copied.

17
00:01:06,189 --> 00:01:07,519
You were not reconstructed.

18
00:01:08,541 --> 00:01:11,691
In fact, this is arguably
not teleportation at all.

19
00:01:12,361 --> 00:01:15,411
It's more like removing the
distance between two places.

20
00:01:16,362 --> 00:01:19,632
In physics, the closest
familiar idea is a wormhole.

21
00:01:20,502 --> 00:01:23,052
A hypothetical shortcut through spacetime.

22
00:01:23,841 --> 00:01:25,531
But there is a small problem.

23
00:01:26,201 --> 00:01:28,971
We don't know how to create
a traversable wormhole.

24
00:01:29,601 --> 00:01:31,531
Or whether one could be kept open.

25
00:01:32,283 --> 00:01:36,243
General relativity allows some
strange geometries of spacetime.

26
00:01:36,323 --> 00:01:41,893
But turning one of them into a practical
doorway is an entirely different problem.

27
00:01:42,685 --> 00:01:45,635
And then there are some
wonderfully inconvenient questions.

28
00:01:46,605 --> 00:01:49,695
What happens if the portal closes
while you're halfway through?

29
00:01:50,441 --> 00:01:52,231
And perhaps most importantly.

30
00:01:52,681 --> 00:01:56,511
How much energy would it take to
manipulate spacetime on this scale?

31
00:01:57,345 --> 00:02:00,175
So the Portal solution
has a major advantage.

32
00:02:01,005 --> 00:02:02,975
You don't have to reconstruct the person.

33
00:02:03,907 --> 00:02:06,837
But instead, you have to do
something even more ridiculous.

34
00:02:07,767 --> 00:02:10,017
You have to manipulate spacetime itself.

35
00:02:12,917 --> 00:02:15,387
Now let's try something
completely different.

36
00:02:16,037 --> 00:02:17,427
The classic transporter.

37
00:02:18,369 --> 00:02:20,899
You enter the machine.
The machine scans you.

38
00:02:21,109 --> 00:02:22,839
Your body is disassembled.

39
00:02:23,289 --> 00:02:25,039
The information is transmitted.

40
00:02:25,449 --> 00:02:27,979
And another machine reconstructs you.

41
00:02:28,626 --> 00:02:30,916
Scan. Data. Transmit. Rebuild.

42
00:02:31,837 --> 00:02:35,527
This sounds like something a
sufficiently advanced computer could do.

43
00:02:35,957 --> 00:02:39,327
But immediately we run into
the first gigantic problem.

44
00:02:40,148 --> 00:02:41,678
What exactly do we scan?

45
00:02:42,488 --> 00:02:45,658
A human body contains roughly
seven octillion atoms.

46
00:02:46,728 --> 00:02:49,758
That's a seven followed by
about twenty-seven zeros.

47
00:02:50,509 --> 00:02:53,159
And knowing where the atoms
are may not be enough.

48
00:02:54,009 --> 00:02:56,499
We also need to know what
those atoms are doing.

49
00:02:57,221 --> 00:02:58,771
What molecules are present?

50
00:02:59,121 --> 00:03:01,651
What are the electrical
states of the neurons?

51
00:03:02,501 --> 00:03:04,711
And how much of the quantum state matters?

52
00:03:05,577 --> 00:03:07,517
The more accurately we
want to reconstruct the

53
00:03:07,597 --> 00:03:10,407
original person, the
more information we need.

54
00:03:11,277 --> 00:03:16,147
And this leads to a rather unpleasant
question. How do we make the scan?

55
00:03:18,687 --> 00:03:20,597
A human being is not a statue.

56
00:03:21,047 --> 00:03:22,417
Especially the brain.

57
00:03:23,087 --> 00:03:25,177
The brain is constantly changing.

58
00:03:25,976 --> 00:03:27,396
Neurons are firing.

59
00:03:27,476 --> 00:03:29,126
Ions are moving.

60
00:03:29,236 --> 00:03:33,086
Membranes are changing
their electrical potentials.

61
00:03:33,671 --> 00:03:38,941
So imagine trying to take a perfect
snapshot of the brain. Not just its shape.

62
00:03:39,331 --> 00:03:42,581
Its physical state. At one exact moment.

63
00:03:43,394 --> 00:03:45,924
So perhaps we should
stop everything first.

64
00:03:46,114 --> 00:03:50,034
Freeze the chemistry. Stop the
brain. Take the measurement.

65
00:03:50,114 --> 00:03:53,124
And then reconstruct the person.

66
00:03:53,987 --> 00:03:56,117
Sounds reasonable. Except...

67
00:03:56,691 --> 00:03:59,711
How do you safely stop a
human brain without destroying

68
00:03:59,791 --> 00:04:02,261
the very information you
are trying to preserve?

69
00:04:03,251 --> 00:04:06,881
We have now built a teleportation
machine whose first step is this.

70
00:04:07,771 --> 00:04:09,771
"Please enter the machine
so we can temporarily

71
00:04:09,851 --> 00:04:12,941
kill all biological
activity in your brain."

72
00:04:13,738 --> 00:04:15,648
Not exactly great marketing.

73
00:04:18,528 --> 00:04:21,218
And even if we solve that,
there is another problem.

74
00:04:21,968 --> 00:04:24,078
How much data is a human being?

75
00:04:24,813 --> 00:04:26,463
It's tempting to say this.

76
00:04:26,713 --> 00:04:30,883
"Just count the atoms and store their
coordinates." But that's not enough.

77
00:04:31,695 --> 00:04:34,345
The amount of information
depends on the precision.

78
00:04:35,335 --> 00:04:39,865
Describe every position to millimetre
accuracy, and that's one amount of data.

79
00:04:41,354 --> 00:04:43,334
Micrometre accuracy? Much more.

80
00:04:43,414 --> 00:04:45,824
Nanometre accuracy? Much more again.

81
00:04:46,589 --> 00:04:48,789
And if you imagine
describing matter down to

82
00:04:48,869 --> 00:04:52,599
extremely tiny scales,
the numbers become absurd.

83
00:04:53,363 --> 00:04:56,493
You might hear people go all the
way down to the Planck length.

84
00:04:57,043 --> 00:04:59,753
But that is not a pixel
size of the universe.

85
00:05:00,663 --> 00:05:03,143
Divide a body into cubes
that size and you get

86
00:05:03,223 --> 00:05:05,773
a number with about a
hundred and three zeros.

87
00:05:06,476 --> 00:05:10,306
And a smaller pixel does not tell
us what information belongs in it.

88
00:05:10,936 --> 00:05:13,646
We aren't taking a
three-dimensional photograph.

89
00:05:14,436 --> 00:05:16,446
We're describing a physical system.

90
00:05:19,066 --> 00:05:22,376
Quantum mechanics makes the
situation even stranger.

91
00:05:23,386 --> 00:05:25,806
We cannot imagine that every
particle has a definite

92
00:05:25,886 --> 00:05:28,836
list of properties that
a scanner can read out.

93
00:05:29,575 --> 00:05:32,325
Measurement itself is
part of quantum mechanics.

94
00:05:32,695 --> 00:05:37,045
And there is a fundamental restriction
known as the no-cloning theorem.

95
00:05:37,915 --> 00:05:41,425
An unknown quantum state cannot
simply be copied perfectly.

96
00:05:42,325 --> 00:05:45,545
Quantum teleportation
does exist experimentally.

97
00:05:45,625 --> 00:05:48,175
But it doesn't teleport matter.

98
00:05:48,785 --> 00:05:53,095
It transfers a quantum state using
entanglement and classical communication.

99
00:05:54,159 --> 00:05:56,569
And it does not leave you
with two perfect copies.

100
00:05:57,159 --> 00:06:02,649
So real quantum teleportation is very
different from a Star Trek transporter.

101
00:06:04,699 --> 00:06:09,029
Let's be generous. Let's assume we
have solved the scanning problem.

102
00:06:09,739 --> 00:06:11,529
We now possess the complete blueprint.

103
00:06:12,564 --> 00:06:15,324
And we send it to another
planet. Congratulations.

104
00:06:15,404 --> 00:06:19,054
We've solved teleportation. Right?

105
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:21,790
No. We now have to build you.

106
00:06:24,540 --> 00:06:27,880
The receiving machine
needs matter. A lot of it.

107
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:29,350
It needs the right elements.

108
00:06:30,249 --> 00:06:34,379
Carbon. Hydrogen. Oxygen.
Nitrogen. And many others.

109
00:06:35,189 --> 00:06:37,239
But having the right atoms isn't enough.

110
00:06:38,069 --> 00:06:41,629
We need the right molecules. The
right proteins. The right organs.

111
00:06:41,709 --> 00:06:44,599
And eventually... the right brain.

112
00:06:45,453 --> 00:06:49,683
It is not enough to manufacture seventy
kilograms of human-shaped matter.

113
00:06:50,533 --> 00:06:53,223
We need to put the right
molecules in the right places.

114
00:06:54,148 --> 00:06:58,138
Notice what the machine cannot
send. It sends information.

115
00:06:58,988 --> 00:07:00,618
It does not send atoms.

116
00:07:01,688 --> 00:07:05,738
So the receiving end needs its own
warehouse of matter, sorted by element.

117
00:07:06,623 --> 00:07:08,233
And then it has to place them.

118
00:07:08,983 --> 00:07:12,613
Suppose the machine puts down
a billion atoms every second.

119
00:07:13,523 --> 00:07:17,273
Seven octillion atoms would take
longer than the universe has existed.

120
00:07:18,137 --> 00:07:20,757
Make it a trillion atoms
a second, and you are

121
00:07:20,837 --> 00:07:23,427
still assembling for two
hundred million years.

122
00:07:24,249 --> 00:07:26,199
And they cannot go down in any order.

123
00:07:26,949 --> 00:07:29,499
Most proteins only work
once they are folded.

124
00:07:30,569 --> 00:07:32,839
A cell only works once it is running.

125
00:07:33,609 --> 00:07:35,259
Much of you is not a structure.

126
00:07:35,949 --> 00:07:39,319
It is a process that has to be
switched on while it is being built.

127
00:07:40,118 --> 00:07:42,168
Imagine the receiving machine saying.

128
00:07:42,718 --> 00:07:45,188
"Okay, I have all the ingredients.

129
00:07:45,598 --> 00:07:48,298
Now I just need to
assemble seven octillion

130
00:07:48,378 --> 00:07:50,588
atoms into one functioning human."

131
00:07:51,269 --> 00:07:54,169
At this point, the teleportation
machine has started looking

132
00:07:54,249 --> 00:07:57,759
suspiciously like the most
advanced factory in the universe.

133
00:07:58,589 --> 00:08:01,659
It is essentially a molecular
three-dimensional printer.

134
00:08:04,819 --> 00:08:07,129
The brain is where it
gets really interesting.

135
00:08:08,119 --> 00:08:10,749
Suppose we successfully
rebuild every neuron.

136
00:08:10,899 --> 00:08:14,449
But one synapse is
wrong. Would you notice?

137
00:08:15,374 --> 00:08:18,184
What if a million synapses
are slightly different?

138
00:08:19,034 --> 00:08:22,744
What if the structure is correct but
some electrical states are different?

139
00:08:23,536 --> 00:08:27,546
What if the memories are preserved but
the exact state of the brain is not?

140
00:08:28,096 --> 00:08:31,726
At what point does the
reconstructed person stop being you?

141
00:08:32,381 --> 00:08:35,211
And this brings us to the
strangest problem of all.

142
00:08:38,361 --> 00:08:40,531
Imagine the transporter scans you.

143
00:08:41,161 --> 00:08:43,831
It creates a perfect
reconstruction on Mars.

144
00:08:44,141 --> 00:08:47,431
But the original is still
standing here on Earth.

145
00:08:48,631 --> 00:08:50,961
Mars-you opens your eyes.
You remember everything.

146
00:08:51,731 --> 00:08:53,681
You remember walking into the transporter.

147
00:08:54,431 --> 00:08:55,801
You remember your family.

148
00:08:56,630 --> 00:09:01,140
You remember pressing the button.
And you say. "Okay. I'm on Mars."

149
00:09:02,061 --> 00:09:06,911
But Earth-you is still standing in the
machine. And you say. "No. I'm on Earth."

150
00:09:07,727 --> 00:09:08,927
Which one is you?

151
00:09:11,977 --> 00:09:16,427
There is no obvious physical experiment
that makes this question go away.

152
00:09:17,237 --> 00:09:19,337
Because both people have exactly the same

153
00:09:19,417 --> 00:09:21,907
history up to the moment
of reconstruction.

154
00:09:22,806 --> 00:09:24,616
Now let's make it even worse.

155
00:09:25,486 --> 00:09:28,456
Suppose the transporter works
by destroying the original.

156
00:09:29,368 --> 00:09:32,098
The receiving machine
builds an identical person.

157
00:09:32,948 --> 00:09:35,338
From the outside,
everything looks perfect.

158
00:09:36,207 --> 00:09:37,787
The new person looks like you.

159
00:09:37,867 --> 00:09:42,217
Talks like you. Remembers your childhood.
Knows your passwords. And says.

160
00:09:43,007 --> 00:09:44,257
"I've been teleported."

161
00:09:45,118 --> 00:09:47,808
But from the perspective of
the original consciousness...

162
00:09:48,618 --> 00:09:50,568
there may have been no journey at all.

163
00:09:51,281 --> 00:09:54,511
You died. And somebody else
woke up believing they were you.

164
00:09:55,217 --> 00:10:00,537
The transporter may therefore be
interpreted as this. Scan. Kill. Copy.

165
00:10:00,617 --> 00:10:02,407
Rather than. Scan. Travel.

166
00:10:03,275 --> 00:10:05,825
And that is not a
technical question anymore.

167
00:10:06,715 --> 00:10:10,145
It is a question about what
personal identity actually means.

168
00:10:12,915 --> 00:10:15,165
Suppose the transporter malfunctions.

169
00:10:16,035 --> 00:10:18,825
Instead of one person
on Mars, it creates two.

170
00:10:19,562 --> 00:10:21,112
Both copies are perfect.

171
00:10:21,922 --> 00:10:23,712
Both remember the same childhood.

172
00:10:24,122 --> 00:10:26,872
Both sincerely believe
they are the original.

173
00:10:27,747 --> 00:10:29,357
Which one is the real you?

174
00:10:31,827 --> 00:10:34,827
And if one of them is
destroyed... did we kill you?

175
00:10:34,907 --> 00:10:36,997
Or did we kill your identical twin?

176
00:10:37,841 --> 00:10:40,201
There may be no objective
answer unless we first

177
00:10:40,281 --> 00:10:43,471
decide what "being the same
person" actually means.

178
00:10:46,381 --> 00:10:51,911
There is yet another possibility. Don't
teleport the body. Teleport the mind.

179
00:10:52,781 --> 00:10:56,371
Scan the brain. Transmit the
information. Run it somewhere else.

180
00:10:57,150 --> 00:10:59,390
Perhaps in another biological brain.

181
00:10:59,470 --> 00:11:01,880
Perhaps in a synthetic brain.

182
00:11:02,390 --> 00:11:03,820
Perhaps in a robot.

183
00:11:04,738 --> 00:11:06,528
Now the question becomes this.

184
00:11:07,178 --> 00:11:08,888
If your mind runs in a machine...

185
00:11:09,078 --> 00:11:10,448
are you still you?

186
00:11:11,138 --> 00:11:15,348
And if we can run two copies
simultaneously... which one is conscious?

187
00:11:15,858 --> 00:11:17,828
Maybe both. Maybe neither.

188
00:11:18,775 --> 00:11:22,105
Maybe consciousness is not
something that can simply be copied.

189
00:11:22,695 --> 00:11:24,225
We don't currently know.

190
00:11:26,785 --> 00:11:28,795
Science fiction has another trick.

191
00:11:29,605 --> 00:11:32,515
Perhaps we temporarily change
how you interact with matter.

192
00:11:33,165 --> 00:11:36,535
You become "out of phase."
You walk through a wall.

193
00:11:37,298 --> 00:11:38,498
Until you ask.

194
00:11:39,298 --> 00:11:42,528
If you don't interact with the wall,
why would you interact with the air?

195
00:11:43,278 --> 00:11:45,048
And why don't you fall through the floor?

196
00:11:45,803 --> 00:11:49,253
If you can selectively interact with
some particles but not others...

197
00:11:49,343 --> 00:11:52,613
we've just invented a
completely new kind of physics.

198
00:11:53,523 --> 00:11:55,513
So again, the idea is simple.

199
00:11:56,383 --> 00:11:57,753
The mechanism is not.

200
00:12:00,253 --> 00:12:03,103
We have now imagined several
very different machines.

201
00:12:04,147 --> 00:12:06,907
The easiest teleportation
to explain is not

202
00:12:06,987 --> 00:12:10,097
necessarily the easiest
teleportation to build.

203
00:12:11,087 --> 00:12:14,377
And the easiest one philosophically
may be the hardest one physically.

204
00:12:15,295 --> 00:12:19,005
A Portal style teleportation
preserves your physical continuity...

205
00:12:19,955 --> 00:12:21,705
but requires control over spacetime.

206
00:12:23,101 --> 00:12:27,291
A Star Trek style transporter may be
easier to imagine technologically...

207
00:12:27,601 --> 00:12:30,741
but creates the horrifying
question of whether

208
00:12:30,821 --> 00:12:33,291
the person who arrives is actually you.

209
00:12:34,001 --> 00:12:37,891
So imagine that tomorrow somebody
offers you a teleportation device.

210
00:12:38,901 --> 00:12:40,991
It has been tested a million times.

211
00:12:41,781 --> 00:12:43,151
Every test is successful.

212
00:12:44,255 --> 00:12:45,755
Every person who comes out is

213
00:12:45,835 --> 00:12:48,465
indistinguishable from
the person who went in.

214
00:12:49,255 --> 00:12:51,125
The machine is perfectly safe.

215
00:12:51,969 --> 00:12:53,259
Except for one thing.

216
00:12:56,279 --> 00:12:58,479
Nobody can prove that
the consciousness on the

217
00:12:58,559 --> 00:13:01,889
other side is the same
consciousness that entered.

218
00:13:02,733 --> 00:13:03,963
Would you use it?

219
00:13:07,098 --> 00:13:10,688
Because perhaps the biggest problem
with teleportation isn't this.

220
00:13:11,138 --> 00:13:14,228
"How do we move a human
from one place to another?"

221
00:13:14,812 --> 00:13:16,012
Perhaps it's this.

222
00:13:16,612 --> 00:13:19,982
"How do we know that the person who
arrives is the person who left?"

223
00:13:20,819 --> 00:13:25,049
And maybe that is the one question no
teleportation machine can ever answer.
